<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>(a) In the proof above, <m>f(1 + 2p)</m> is a multiple of <m>p</m> ‘in the same way’ as <m>f(1 + p)</m>. Find the other factor, in terms of <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> and <m>p</m>.</p>
<div><m>f(1 + 2p) = p \times</m> ( <fillin characters="20" name="ans1"/> )  </div>
<p>(b) The proof needs <m>f(n)</m> to be a multiple of <m>p</m> at three values of <m>n</m>, not just two. To see why, give an example of a quadratic <m>f(n)</m>, with whole-number coefficients, for which <m>f(1) = {3}</m> and <m>f({4}) = {3}</m>.</p>
<div><m>f(n) =</m> <fillin characters="20" name="ans2"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) Expand, and use <m>a + b + c = p</m> to replace the terms left over:</p>
<me>f(1 + 2p) = a(1 + 4p + 4p^2) + b(1 + 2p) + c = 4ap^2 + 4ap + 2bp + (a + b + c) = 4ap^2 + 4ap + 2bp + p = p(4ap + 4a + 2b + 1).</me>
<p>(b) <m>f(1) = f({4}) = {3}</m> means that <m>f(n) - {3}</m> has the roots 1 and <m>{4}</m>, so <m>f(n) = k(n - 1)(n - {4}) + {3}</m> for any whole number <m>k \neq 0</m>; for <m>k = 1</m>, <m>f(n) = {n^2-5 n+7}</m>. So a quadratic can equal <m>{3}</m> at two values of <m>n</m>. It cannot at three, because <m>f(n) - {3} = 0</m> is a quadratic equation, with at most two solutions. For <m>k = 1</m>, <m>f({7}) = {21} = {3} \times {7}</m>, which is not prime: exactly what the proof predicts.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>4 a p+2 b+4 a+1</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>n^2-5 n+7</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>